# A stub makes `this` for a plain function before the function has a frame, and reading `prototype` of new.target may run any code. A plain function that may be entered by value has a row that says where a stack trace lists it meanwhile, in front of the rows of its calls in tail position, and a call at the same place keeps its own row. One call beside it is still the only call, so it names no callee. An arrow function, a function that is only called directly and a function that stack traces hide have none
# RUN: tables functions

module m0 path="main.js" displayPath="main.js" body=f0 positions={lineStarts=[0, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600] whereThisIsMade=[100, 120, 200, 240, 300, 320, 400, 420, 500, 520]}
entry module=m0
